WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.030
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:07.230
A convex quadrilateral is divided into four parts by its diagonals

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.620
Three of the areas are two, one, and three as shown in the diagram

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:19.499
What is the area of the fourth region denoted by x?

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:23.938
I want to thank Gerrit for sending me this problem and a solution

00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:32.008
Can you figure it out? Give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching the video for a solution

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.020
Rather than solving this specific problem,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:47.720
I will solve a more general problem in which the four areas are s1, s2, s3, and s4

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.800
I'll label the vertices of the quadrilateral as A, B, C and D,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.680
and I'll mark the point at which the diagonals meet as E

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.980
In order to write formulas for each of these areas,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.480
we will draw altitudes from vertices A and C to diagonal BD

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.880
From vertex A, for example, we'll draw an altitude which has a height of h1

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.500
From vertex C, we'll draw another altitude which has a height of h2

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:29.920
We can now use the formula for the area of a triangle to figure out the areas of each of these regions

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.920
For example,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:38.960
s1 is the area of a triangle, which has a base of DE and a height of h1

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.100
So s1 will equal DE times h1, divided by 2

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:52.660
Similarly, s2 is the area of a triangle which has a base of BE and a height of h1

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:57.840
so s2 equals BE times h1 divided by 2

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:06.000
We can, continue writing the formulas for the areas of these regions using the same principle

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:15.400
Now if you look at these formulas and perhaps rearrange these equations, you'll notice something familiar in these variables

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:24.680
The variables in the first two sets of equations are exactly the same variables in the next two sets of equations

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:33.200
In fact, if you multiply the first two equations, you would get exactly the same result as if you multiplied the third and fourth equations

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:40.640
So we end up with the principle that s1 times s3 equals s2 times s4

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.060
We can now take this formula and use the specific numbers from our original problem to solve for x

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:56.960
We substitute in our values and we end up with x is equal to 6

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Did you figure it out?

